# -*- mode:org;coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: alfe-autocad-vbscript-comments — generated AutoCAD bridge starts with invalid VBScript comments
#+STATUS: TODO
#+PRIORITY: P1
#+TYPE: cad-runtime
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: fix-alfe-autocad-vbscript-comments

* Context

ALFE 1.8.3 uses a generated =bridge-autocad.vbs= to start the full
AutoCAD application through COM when the AutoCAD backend is selected in
automation mode.  This is the path selected, for example, by:

#+begin_src sh
alfe -norc --autocad --cad acad-2022 --mode automation \
  --dwg drawing.dwg --keep-workdir --print-command
#+end_src

The full application is required by CAD tests which use ActiveX.  The
batch alternative selects =accoreconsole.exe=, where functions such as
=vlax-get-acad-object= and =vlax-ename->vla-object= are unavailable, so
it is not a workaround for those tests.

* Problem

The bridge emitted by ALFE 1.8.3 begins with Lisp-style comments:

#+begin_src vbscript
;; AutoCAD COM bridge — emitted by alfe.backend.autocad
;; Placeholders: ...
;; auto, , 60.
;; Mirrors the legacy bash wrapper's bridge-autocad.vbs; preserve
;; the WaitQuiescent + GetAcadState handshake — it's the hard-won
;; piece that keeps the bridge from racing AutoCAD's UI init.
Option Explicit
#+end_src

=;;= is not a VBScript comment marker.  VBScript accepts an apostrophe
(='=) or =Rem=, and consequently =cscript= rejects the generated file
before any COM or AutoCAD initialization takes place:

#+begin_example
bridge-autocad.vbs(1, 1) Erreur de compilation Microsoft VBScript: Instruction attendue
alfe[warn]: backend AUTOCAD: process exited after 0.16 s; last status = "BOOTING"
alfe: alfe AUTOCAD backend error (phase BOOTSTRAP): AutoCAD process exited before READY (exit 1) [PROCESS-EXITED-BEFORE-READY]
#+end_example

The invalid text comes from the VBScript template in
=autolisp-front-end/source/backend-autocad.lisp= (the template beginning
near line 252), not from the generated work directory or the installed
AutoCAD version.

* Reproduction

1. Install ALFE 1.8.3 on Windows with AutoCAD 2022 available.
2. Run the command above with an existing DWG and inspect the command
   printed for automation mode.  It invokes:

   #+begin_example
   cscript //nologo C:/msys64/tmp/alfe-autocad-.../bridge-autocad.vbs
   #+end_example

3. Run the command without =--print-command=, or invoke =cscript=
   directly on the retained bridge.  Compilation fails at line 1,
   column 1 with =Instruction attendue=.
4. Inspect the retained file: its first bytes are =;; AutoCAD COM
   bridge= and the first valid VBScript statement, =Option Explicit=,
   occurs only after six invalid comment lines.

The same failure is visible from SCHMS CAD tests run through ALFE with
=--autocad --cad acad-2022 --mode automation=.

* Cause

The string template in =backend-autocad.lisp= was copied with Common
Lisp/AutoLISP =;;= comment syntax.  ALFE writes that string verbatim to
a =.vbs= file.  No translation step changes the comment delimiters, so
the Windows Script Host parser sees the first semicolon as an invalid
instruction.

* Proposal

1. Change the introductory lines in the generated VBScript template to
   use apostrophe comments (or =Rem=) while leaving the bridge protocol,
   =WaitQuiescent= and =GetAcadState= logic unchanged.
2. Add a regression test for the emitted bridge.  At minimum, assert
   that every introductory comment uses valid VBScript syntax and that
   the generated file begins with syntactically valid VBScript.  On a
   Windows test lane, ask =cscript //nologo= to compile/start the bridge
   far enough to prove that it no longer reports a line-1 compilation
   error.
3. Exercise the real backend with the explicit selector
   =--cad acad-2022 --mode automation=; do not validate only batch mode.

* Acceptance criteria

- A newly emitted =bridge-autocad.vbs= contains no =;;= comment prefix
  and is accepted by the VBScript parser.
- =alfe -norc --autocad --cad acad-2022 --mode automation ...= gets
  beyond bridge compilation and can reach the backend =READY= state on
  a Windows host with AutoCAD 2022 installed.
- An automated regression test fails if Lisp comment syntax is emitted
  into the VBScript template again.
- AutoCAD batch-mode command selection and the existing bridge protocol
  are unchanged.

* Status 2026-08-14 — FIXED IN CODE (clautolisp 1.8.33), one criterion open

Two copies of this ticket existed for a few hours: this one, pushed from
Windows, and a shorter write-up filed in =issues/closed/= when the code was
fixed earlier the same evening. They are merged here, keeping THIS text — it
carries the real =cscript= error, the exact selector to reproduce with, and
the acceptance criteria, none of which the other had. The =issues/closed/=
copy is deleted.

It stays OPEN because one acceptance criterion is genuinely unmet. Checking
them off in order:

1. [X] No =;;= prefix, accepted by the VBScript parser — the template's
   comment lines now use an apostrophe.
2. [ ] =alfe --autocad --cad acad-2022 --mode automation= reaching =READY=
   on a Windows host with AutoCAD 2022 — *NOT verified.* It needs the
   Windows runner with AutoCAD, which no job could reach: the CAD-capable
   jobs were tagged =bricscad= / =autocad=, tags NO runner carries (checked
   through the API: zero for both). Fixed in the same release — one =cad=
   tag on the concurrency=1 runner, per pjb — so those jobs can now be
   scheduled, but the run itself has not happened.
3. [X] A regression test that fails if Lisp comment syntax comes back —
   three tests in =autolisp-front-end/tests/backend-cad-tests.lisp=,
   asserted on the EMITTED FILE rather than on the template constant (what
   cscript parses is the file, and a substitution could in principle
   introduce a line the template never had). One of the three proves the
   detector fires on both defects it is meant to catch: a check that cannot
   fail is worth nothing.
4. [X] Batch-mode selection and the bridge protocol unchanged — only
   comment lines changed; WaitQuiescent / GetAcadState / SendCommand are
   untouched and their pinning test still passes.

** Two findings beyond the report

- *BricsCAD carried the identical defect*, in the identical position — its
  header is a copy of this one. Unreported because the BricsCAD automation
  path on Windows has not been exercised (macOS does not use VBScript, and
  =verify:bricscad-hidden-ui:windows= drives PowerShell rather than the
  bridge). Fixing only the reported one would have left a live copy of the
  same bug behind a lane that is about to start running. Both are fixed.
- *The headers also carried an em dash*, and =EMIT-BRIDGE-VBS= writes the
  file as UTF-8 WITHOUT a BOM while cscript reads a =.vbs= as ANSI unless
  one is present. Inside a comment that is only ugly — but it is one edit
  away from being inside code, and there was a second em dash further down.
  Both headers are ASCII now, with the reason recorded so a typographic
  dash is not reintroduced. Same class of trap as the comment marker: a
  property of how the RECEIVING tool parses the file, invisible from the
  Lisp side.

** Status 2026-08-23 — criterion-2 run reached the bridge; next blocker fixed (alfe 1.9.8)

The first real automation run on the Windows/AutoCAD-2022 host (alfe 1.9.7,
=--mode automation --cad acad-2022=) got PAST bridge compilation — the =;;=
and PROBE crashes are gone — and died inside the emitted VBScript:

: bridge-autocad.vbs(72, 1) Microsoft VBScript: Objet requis (err 424)
: AutoCAD process exited before READY (exit 0)  [PROCESS-EXITED-BEFORE-READY]

Emitted line 72 = =backend-autocad.lisp= ~=If app Is Nothing Then=. Cause:
=app= is Dim'd but never initialised. =commode= defaults to ="auto"=, so the
GetObject attach block runs; with no AutoCAD already open, =Set app =
GetObject(...)= fails under =On Error Resume Next= and leaves =app= *Empty*
(not an object). The following =If app Is Nothing= is outside the
error-suppression scope, and =Empty Is Nothing= raises err 424 — so the
script dies instead of falling through to =CreateObject=. BricsCAD carried
the identical trap (=backend-bricscad.lisp=). Fixed by initialising =Set app
= Nothing= / =Set doc = Nothing= up front in BOTH templates, with a
regression test (emitted VBS must init =app= before its first =Is Nothing=).
Fix in alfe 1.9.8.

Criterion 2 STILL OPEN: this removes the current wall but only a real-AutoCAD
run can confirm =READY= (there may be further steps after =CreateObject=).

** Next step

On alfe 1.9.8, re-run =alfe --autocad --cad acad-2022 --mode automation
--dwg ... --keep-workdir --on-error debug= on the =cad= runner: it should now
launch AutoCAD via =CreateObject= and progress toward READY. If a later step
fails, =--on-error debug= gives the backtrace and =--keep-workdir= the
emitted bridge. A confirmed READY + =--eval= round-trip closes criterion 2
and the ticket.
